<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/advanced/scripture-and-interpretation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Scripture is <strong>text plus community plus interpretive method</strong> — the three are
    inseparable. How a tradition selects its canon, authorises its interpreters and constrains
    (or liberates) its readings determines what the tradition can become. This lesson is the
    hermeneutics: how sacred texts are actually read.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="canon, interpretive styles, fundamentalism as modernity, translation" widget="equationStep" config={scriptCfg} />

  <h2>The interpretive spectrum, mapped</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Jewish midrash and Talmud:</strong> interpretation as endless, sanctified argument — questions preserved, not erased. The model other traditions cite.</li>
    <li><strong>Christian historical criticism:</strong> from Spinoza to the Jesus Seminar — reading the Bible as documents with dates, sources and agendas. Transformed belief for some, deepened it for others, scandalised most at first.</li>
    <li><strong>Islamic tafsir and ijtihad:</strong> layered commentary (Tabari, Razi) and independent reasoning — with the "closing of the gates of ijtihad" debate (historians dispute that the gates ever fully closed).</li>
    <li><strong>Why it matters beyond religion:</strong> constitutional originalism vs living-document readings are scripture-interpretation debates in legal dress — hermeneutics is everyone's subject, whether they know it or not.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Barton, Holy Writings, Sacred Text; primary commentaries (Rashi, Tabari); Armstrong, The Battle for God (2000).</p>
</TopicPage>
